{
  "_comment": "The cross-language node-registry parity fixture (TASK-734 Task 3, extended by TASK-720 Task 5 with the five summarization-palette node types, by TASK-724 Task 3 with the eight STT-palette node types — stt.phiHop is implemented:false, a documented placeholder pending TASK-710 — and by TASK-731 Task 9 with the consultation palette. The consultation palette now carries ALL THIRTEEN node types of docs/implementation/TASK-731-Palette-Consultation/contracts/node-types.md's node table: TASK-731 shipped three (consentGate, phiHop, hitlGate) and left ten specified-but-unwired, which made the palette unbuildable because DRAFT_CONSULTATION_RULE_SET names nine node types by key; the remaining ten interpreter wrappers were added later over the compile targets palette-contract.md §1 had already verified. consultation.hitlGate remains implemented:false, a documented placeholder pending the interpreter's own durable-wait extension (Phase B), so compile() still refuses any graph containing it. Asserted from BOTH packages/workflow-contract/src/__tests__/node-registry-parity.test.ts (TS) and apps/harness/src/harness/tests/unit/temporal/interpreter/test_node_registry_parity.py (Python) — see packages/workflow-contract/src/node-registry.ts's module docstring for why the two sides check against this file instead of against each other. Also carries the two palette-agnostic GRAPH BOUNDARY MARKERS `core.start`/`core.end` (TS class `boundary`): the palette-independent structural rules WF-S-002/003/004/007 are written against these literal node types, so until they were registered no graph in any palette could validate — the platform's own seeded summarization graph scored 17 errors against its own validator. They execute nothing but are `implemented: true` because compile() refuses any graph containing an unimplemented type. `entries` sorted by `key`. Only fields BOTH registries carry today are listed here; `classes`/`paletteKey` (TS-only) and `kind` (Python-only) are intentionally excluded. Extended by TASK-791 with R3's three missing capabilities — consultation.realtimeSummary, consultation.suggestions and consultation.proposeCorrections — which TASK-789 verified had no node, activity or sensor anywhere. realtimeSummary is externalWrite:true because it publishes to the live consultation feed (so a sandboxed run skips it); suggestions and proposeCorrections are externalWrite:false because both are PROPOSAL surfaces that write nothing — proposeCorrections in particular must never be the thing that edits clinical text. Extended by TASK-809 OD-15 (option A) with `outputKeys`: the ONE port field that is deliberately SHARED rather than TS-only. A port NAME is an authoring handle (`out`, `entities`, `verdict`) — what the canvas draws and what a graph edge's fromPort/toPort names — but the interpreter threads values by reading a KEY out of the producing activity's own NodeActivityResult.output dict, and no activity has ever emitted a key called \"out\". Before OD-15 the only bridge was the interpreter's whole-object fallback, which is exactly the untyped bundle the port vocabulary exists to abolish. `outputKeys` maps EVERY declared output port name to the key it carries, or to null for a `control` port, which carries no payload at all — that null is what lets _resolve_bound_inputs tell a legitimate ordering edge apart from an edge naming a port that does not exist (the latter raises). Asserted from BOTH sides exactly like every other field here; adding it to one language's projection without the other is a failing test, not a silent drift. Extended by TASK-812 with the ENDPOINT STAGE's three node types — session.timeout, summary.finalize and feedback.capture — the ordered sequence that runs before a consultation session closes. All three are trigger:'on-end', lane:'durable' and therefore idempotent (TS-only fields; the durable-lane idempotency rule is enforced by nodeDescriptorContractProblems, not by this fixture). All three are externalWrite:true: session.timeout stamps the consultation's endpoint disposition, summary.finalize LOCKS every document of the consultation (DD-3, not just the SOAP note), and feedback.capture promotes an ACCEPTED advisory transcript correction over the raw channel (DD-8 — the only path that can; consultation.proposeCorrections stays externalWrite:false and never applies anything). Their outputKeys carry the control port only: each one's product is a state change, not a value, exactly like output.deliver.",
